QUANTLET_INTROS = {'MFM_ch18_bank_indices': ('# MFM_ch18_bank_indices\n\nEqual-weighted, daily rebalanced portfolios of US, European and Romanian banks, 2010-2026, with the largest peak-to-trough falls and annualised volatilities.\n\nReturns are measured between consecutive dates common to all banks and indices, after aligning prices. A gap can therefore span more than one trading day. Constituent returns are in local currencies without FX conversion; the European basket mixes currencies. The value 100 is invested before the first displayed return; the first point already includes that return. peak_date is the all-time index maximum, not the peak preceding the largest drawdown. vol is the standard deviation of the equal-weighted mean log return, scaled by √252, a 252-observation convention on this common calendar. The wealth curve instead compounds the equal-weighted simple return. The Romanian basket contains TLV and BRD.\n\n*Modelling Financial Markets (MFM), Chapter 18: Systemic Risk and Networks. Author: Daniel Traian Pele.*', '# MFM_ch18_bank_indices\n\nEqual-weighted, daily rebalanced portfolios of US, European and Romanian banks, 2010-2026, with the largest peak-to-trough falls and annualised volatilities.\n\n*Modelling Financial Markets (MFM), Chapter 18: Systemic Risk and Networks. Author: Daniel Traian Pele.*\n\nReturns are measured between consecutive dates common to all banks and indices, after aligning prices. A gap can therefore span more than one trading day. Constituent returns are in local currencies without FX conversion; the European basket mixes currencies. The value 100 is invested before the first displayed return; the first point already includes that return. peak_date is the all-time index maximum, not the peak preceding the largest drawdown. vol is the standard deviation of the equal-weighted mean log return, scaled by √252, a 252-observation convention on this common calendar. The wealth curve instead compounds the equal-weighted simple return. The Romanian basket contains TLV and BRD.'), 'MFM_ch18_covar': ('# MFM_ch18_covar\n\nCoVaR and Delta-CoVaR at 1% for eleven US and European banks by quantile regression (system: the other banks of the region), moving-block bootstrap intervals, VaR versus Delta-CoVaR and exposure CoVaR.\n\nReturns are measured between consecutive dates common to all banks and indices, after aligning prices. A gap can therefore span more than one trading day. Constituent returns are in local currencies without FX conversion; the European basket mixes currencies. ΔCoVaR is the additional conditional loss relative to the median state, not the total conditional loss CoVaR. System and exposure bars have different outcomes: the other-bank portfolio versus the individual bank. Their levels are not directly comparable measures of strength. The pooled Spearman correlation is descriptive and combines regional systems. Marginal bootstrap intervals do not supply pairwise or simultaneous tests of the ranking; overlapping intervals alone do not prove equal effects.\n\n*Modelling Financial Markets (MFM), Chapter 18: Systemic Risk and Networks. Author: Daniel Traian Pele.*', '# MFM_ch18_covar\n\nCoVaR and Delta-CoVaR at 1% for eleven US and European banks by quantile regression (system: the other banks of the region), moving-block bootstrap intervals, VaR versus Delta-CoVaR and exposure CoVaR.\n\n*Modelling Financial Markets (MFM), Chapter 18: Systemic Risk and Networks. Author: Daniel Traian Pele.*\n\nReturns are measured between consecutive dates common to all banks and indices, after aligning prices. A gap can therefore span more than one trading day. Constituent returns are in local currencies without FX conversion; the European basket mixes currencies. ΔCoVaR is the additional conditional loss relative to the median state, not the total conditional loss CoVaR. System and exposure bars have different outcomes: the other-bank portfolio versus the individual bank. Their levels are not directly comparable measures of strength. The pooled Spearman correlation is descriptive and combines regional systems. Marginal bootstrap intervals do not supply pairwise or simultaneous tests of the ranking; overlapping intervals alone do not prove equal effects.'), 'MFM_ch18_covar_dynamic': ('# MFM_ch18_covar_dynamic\n\nTime-varying Delta-CoVaR at 1% with lagged state variables (log VIX, regional index and system returns) for six US and European banks, 2010-2026.\n\nReturns are measured between consecutive dates common to all banks and indices, after aligning prices. A gap can therefore span more than one trading day. Constituent returns are in local currencies without FX conversion; the European basket mixes currencies. The graph shows a 21-observation moving average; the table describes the unsmoothed observations, including their maximum. The loading b is constant: time variation comes from the conditional quantile spread of the individual bank, multiplied by b, rather than a changing transmission coefficient. VIX is a common state variable for US and European banks. The time average of dynamic ΔCoVaR need not equal the static estimate.\n\n*Modelling Financial Markets (MFM), Chapter 18: Systemic Risk and Networks. Author: Daniel Traian Pele.*', '# MFM_ch18_covar_dynamic\n\nTime-varying Delta-CoVaR at 1% with lagged state variables (log VIX, regional index and system returns) for six US and European banks, 2010-2026.\n\n*Modelling Financial Markets (MFM), Chapter 18: Systemic Risk and Networks. Author: Daniel Traian Pele.*\n\nReturns are measured between consecutive dates common to all banks and indices, after aligning prices. A gap can therefore span more than one trading day. Constituent returns are in local currencies without FX conversion; the European basket mixes currencies. The graph shows a 21-observation moving average; the table describes the unsmoothed observations, including their maximum. The loading b is constant: time variation comes from the conditional quantile spread of the individual bank, multiplied by b, rather than a changing transmission coefficient. VIX is a common state variable for US and European banks. The time average of dynamic ΔCoVaR need not equal the static estimate.'), 'MFM_ch18_covar_tests': ('# MFM_ch18_covar_tests\n\nInference for systemic-risk measures of 11 US and European banks: joint moving-block bootstrap (999 draws) tests of Delta-CoVaR = 0, of equal tail and median slopes and of equal contributions (Holm correction, joint Wald test), the Euler identity ES = the weighted sum of MES when conditioning on the portfolio itself, the Girardi-Ergun CoVaR at 5% and an out-of-sample backtest of its joint hits.\n\n*Modelling Financial Markets (MFM), Chapter 18: Systemic Risk and Networks. Author: Daniel Traian Pele.*', '# MFM_ch18_covar_tests\n\nInference for systemic-risk measures of 11 US and European banks: joint moving-block bootstrap (999 draws) tests of Delta-CoVaR = 0, of equal tail and median slopes and of equal contributions (Holm correction, joint Wald test), the Euler identity ES = the weighted sum of MES when conditioning on the portfolio itself, the Girardi-Ergun CoVaR at 5% and an out-of-sample backtest of its joint hits.\n\n*Modelling Financial Markets (MFM), Chapter 18: Systemic Risk and Networks. Author: Daniel Traian Pele.*\n\nThe Euler check is an algebraic allocation identity on the same portfolio-tail observations: ES = Σ w_i MES_i, with six equal weights w_i = 1/6. Conditioning MES on the S&P 500 instead changes the event and need not reproduce portfolio ES. In the bank table, lo and hi are marginal 95% bootstrap endpoints for ΔCoVaR; b and b50 are the tail and median slopes, db = b − b50. The slope p-values use a Normal approximation with bootstrap standard errors, not Monte Carlo exceedance counts. Holm controls the family of 25 within-region pair comparisons jointly, whereas each Wald test concerns one region; their conclusions need not coincide. Girardi–Ergün conditions on the bank return being below its VaR, unlike the point-at-VaR conditioning of the quantile-regression calculation. The backtest tests a joint hit rate of α² and separately the marginal bank hit rate α. Non-rejection of the joint rate alone does not validate conditional coverage. Only about seven joint hits are expected in this sample, so asymptotic p-values and estimated tail quantiles require caution.'), 'MFM_ch18_frm': ('# MFM_ch18_frm\n\nFinancial Risk Meter for 13 banks: L1-penalized quantile regression at 5% on 63-day windows, penalty chosen by generalized approximate cross-validation (GACV); GACV curves in a calm and a crisis window, the average penalty over time and the share of active predictors (bank and macro) at a fixed penalty.\n\n*Modelling Financial Markets (MFM), Chapter 18: Systemic Risk and Networks. Author: Daniel Traian Pele.*', "# MFM_ch18_frm\n\nFinancial Risk Meter for 13 banks: L1-penalized quantile regression at 5% on 63-day windows, penalty chosen by generalized approximate cross-validation (GACV); GACV curves in a calm and a crisis window, the average penalty over time and the share of active predictors (bank and macro) at a fixed penalty.\n\n*Modelling Financial Markets (MFM), Chapter 18: Systemic Risk and Networks. Author: Daniel Traian Pele.*\n\nThe penalty is selected from a discrete logarithmic grid; different selected grid points do not by themselves establish a precisely measured difference in risk. Returns enter as fractions without regressor standardisation, so penalty levels depend on the scale of the inputs. The 5% quantile regression uses all 63 observations, but information about the extreme tail is limited. Bank regressors are contemporaneous and market closing times differ; the fitted links describe conditional association, not causal transmission. The FRM–VIX correlation pairs each window's FRM with VIX at its endpoint. Density counts nonzero coefficients among 12 other banks and three lagged market variables for each equation.\n\nFor each window, FRM is the arithmetic mean of the 13 bank-specific penalty parameters λ selected by minimizing GACV on the stated grid. When GACV values at several grid points are numerically almost equal, very small numerical differences can change the selected penalty and hence FRM, even when the regression coefficients are almost unchanged. The code selects the grid point with the smallest computed GACV; it does not merge near-ties or impose a tolerance-based choice. Small FRM differences between runs in different computing environments should therefore be interpreted alongside the GACV curves and the scaling of returns and regressors, rather than as precisely measured changes in systemic risk.\n"), 'MFM_ch18_mes_srisk': ('# MFM_ch18_mes_srisk\n\nMarginal Expected Shortfall (MES 5%) of 13 banks against their market index with moving-block bootstrap intervals, long-run MES and the break-even leverage above which SRISK is positive.\n\n*Modelling Financial Markets (MFM), Chapter 18: Systemic Risk and Networks. Author: Daniel Traian Pele.*', "# MFM_ch18_mes_srisk\n\nMarginal Expected Shortfall (MES 5%) of 13 banks against their market index with moving-block bootstrap intervals, long-run MES and the break-even leverage above which SRISK is positive.\n\n*Modelling Financial Markets (MFM), Chapter 18: Systemic Risk and Networks. Author: Daniel Traian Pele.*\n\nMES 5% conditions on the regional market's own 5% quantile; the MES used in the LRMES shortcut instead conditions on a fixed market return below −2%. These events need not have the same probability across markets. The displayed MES and es5_m are percentages. The lrmes helper receives the −2%-threshold MES in percent and divides it by 100 inside the exponent: a value of 3 becomes 0.03, giving 1 − exp(−18 × 0.03) ≈ 0.417; es5_m is the market's Expected Shortfall and is therefore repeated within a region. LRMES is a fraction. The SRISK-to-equity formula here is the untruncated capital gap: negative values indicate a surplus, not a negative positive-part capital requirement."), 'MFM_ch18_quantile_connectedness': ('# MFM_ch18_quantile_connectedness\n\nCase study of Ando, Greenwood-Nimmo and Shin (2022), quantile connectedness of 17 sovereign and 17 financial-sector CDS series, 2006-2017: the published full-sample spillover index of the quantile factor VAR by quantile (Figure 4) against the conditional-mean index, and the published correlations of the rolling tail indices (Table 3) and of the tail indices and relative tail dependence with CATFIN (Figure 13).\n\n*Modelling Financial Markets (MFM), Chapter 18: Systemic Risk and Networks. Author: Daniel Traian Pele.*', '# MFM_ch18_quantile_connectedness\n\nCase study of Ando, Greenwood-Nimmo and Shin (2022), quantile connectedness of 17 sovereign and 17 financial-sector CDS series, 2006-2017: the published full-sample spillover index of the quantile factor VAR by quantile (Figure 4) against the conditional-mean index, and the published correlations of the rolling tail indices (Table 3) and of the tail indices and relative tail dependence with CATFIN (Figure 13).\n\n*Modelling Financial Markets (MFM), Chapter 18: Systemic Risk and Networks. Author: Daniel Traian Pele.*\n\nCDS means credit default swap; QFVAR means quantile factor vector autoregression. CATFIN (CATastrophic risk in the FINancial sector) averages three 1% VaR estimates for financial stocks. These figures display published reference values, not a new fit. The central segment at about 40% summarises an approximate range and is not an estimated flat quantile curve; lines between the six labelled tail points are visual guides. RTD is the difference of two spillover percentages, in percentage points. Source: [Ando, Greenwood-Nimmo and Shin (2022), Figure 4, Table 3 and Figure 13](https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.2021.3984).')}

